            "\\section{Related work}\n",
            "Leveraging human- and machine-generated natural language (NL) feedback for refining outputs has been effective for a variety of tasks, including summarization \\cite{scheurerandperez2022training}, script generation \\cite{tandon2021interscript}, program synthesis \\cite{Le2022CodeRL, Yasunaga2020DrRepair}, \n",
            "and other tasks \\cite{bai2022rlhf, Schick2022PEERAC, Saunders2022SelfCritique, bai2022constitutional, Welleck2022SelfCorrect}. Refinement methods differ in the source and format of feedback, and the way that a refiner is obtained.\n",
            "Table \\ref{tab:related_work_short_summary} summarizes some related approaches; see \\Cref{app:related} for an additional discussion.\n",
            "\n",
            "\n",
            "\n",
            " \n",
            "\n",
            "\\paragraph{Source of feedback.}\n",
            "\n",
            "Humans have been an effective source of feedback \\cite{tandon2021interscript,elgohary2021nl,tandon2022learning,bai2022rlhf}. \n",
            "Since human feedback is costly, several approaches\n",
            "use a scalar reward function as a surrogate of (or alternative to) human feedback~(e.g., \\cite{bai2022rlhf, Liu2022RainierRK, Lu2022QuarkCT, Le2022CodeRL, Welleck2022SelfCorrect}).\n",
            "Alternative sources such as compilers \n",
            "\\cite{Yasunaga2020DrRepair} or Wikipedia edits \\cite{Schick2022PEERAC} can provide domain-specific feedback.\n",
            "Recently, LLMs have been used to generate feedback for general domains \\cite{fu2023gptscore,Peng2023LLMAugmenter,Yang2022Re3GL},\n",
            "However, ours \n",
            "is the only method that generates\n",
            "feedback using an \\llm on its \\emph{own} output, for the purpose of refining with the same \\llm.\n",
            "\n",
            "\n",
