            "\n",
            "\\end{itemize}\n",
            "\n",
            "Recall that the Base LLM requires a generation prompt $p_{gen}$ with input-output pairs $\\langle x_i, y_i \\rangle$, the feedback module requires a feedback prompt $p_{fb}$ with input-output-feedback triples $\\langle x_i, y_i, fb_i \\rangle$, and the refinement module (\\itermod) requires a refinement prompt $p_{refine}$ with input-output-feedback-refined quadruples $\\langle x_i, y_i, fb_i, y_{i+1} \\rangle$.\n",
            "\n",
            "\n",
            "\\begin{itemize}\n",
            "    \\item \\textbf{\\sentxfer} We create positive and negative variants of a single review from the training set and manually write a description for converting the negative variant to positive and vice versa. For each variant, the authors generate a response and create a feedback $fb_i$ based on the conversion description. \n",
            "\\item \n",
            "\\textbf{\\dialgen} \n",
            "We sample six examples as $\\langle x_i, y_i \\rangle$ for the few-shot prompt for the Base LLM. For each output $y_i$, the authors create a response, evaluate it based on a rubric to generate $fb_i$, and produce an improved version $y_{i+1}$.\n",
            "\n",
            "\\item \\textbf{\\acrogen} We provide the Base LLM with a total of 15 (title, acronym) examples. \n",
            "Then, for one title ($x_i$) we generate an acronym ($y_i$) using \\chatgpt.\n",
            "The authors then score the acronyms based on a 5-point rubric to create the corresponding $fb_i$, and write improved versions of the acronym to create $y_{i+1}$.\n",
            "3 such examples are used for \\itermod and \\fbmod.\n",
            "\n",
            "\\item \\textbf{\\codeopt} We use the slow ($x_i$) and fast ($y_i$) versions of programs released by \\citet{pie} for Base LLM.\n",
            "We use their provided explanations \\citep{pie} for \\fbmod and \\itermod.\n",
            "\n",
            "\\item \\textbf{\\gsm} The prompts for the Base LLM are sourced from PaL~\\citep{gao2022pal} as $\\langle x_i, y_i \\rangle$. \n",
